+ 36. STRING AND THE NUMBERS ARE VALUE-BASED (Phase 9b1). OWNER: none.
+
+     The rule is section 1 of
+     docs/superpowers/specs/2026-10-14-phase-9b1-uniform-object-model.md,
+     approved by Moritz 2026-10-14. A value-based value is one whose class is
+     `.String`, with no subclass and no enhancement: every string, every
+     number, `.true` and `.false`.
+
+     NOT SPECIFIED for a value-based value:
+       - whether two equal values are one object, in both directions
+         (Deviation 4), seen through `identityHash`, an IdentityTable or
+         IdentitySet key, or an operation that answers its receiver
+         unchanged;
+       - whether `~copy` answers the same object or another;
+       - whether a WeakReference to one clears after the program drops its
+         last reference to it.
+     The oracle's per-package literal pool and its -10..100 integer cache
+     are its implementation, not semantics.
+
+     STILL SPECIFIED:
+       - a held value's `identityHash` is stable through reads, an alias,
+         an argument, a return, and storage in and retrieval from a
+         collection;
+       - a WeakReference to a value never clears while the value is
+         reachable;
+       - a subclass instance (`.MyString~new`) and an enhanced string are
+         ordinary objects with full identity, and accept `objectName=` and
+         every other per-object operation.
+     The first and third are witnessed on both interpreters by
+     corpus/lang/value_based_identity.rex.
+
+     A value-based value carries no per-object state, so `objectName=` on
+     one is refused, rc 120, "OBJECTNAME= on a String is refused because a
+     String is value-based (Deviation 36)". Moritz: "I don't think object
+     name is an important API." The oracle keeps the name in the object its
+     construction path chose, so the values that answer it are the ones its
+     identities merge. Measured, probes `objname2` and `objname4` (Phase 9a
+     Task 8b report, section 1), oracle rc 0, kept with `objname-table.rex`
+     in docs/superpowers/records/2026-10-11-phase-9a/deviation-probes/:
+
+       `longs = 'a longer string than seven'; longs~objectName = 'long'`
+       `'a longer string than seven'~objectName`        long
+       `('a longer string' || ' than seven')~objectName`  a String
+       `('a longer string than seven' || '')~objectName`  long
+       the same literal in a `::ROUTINE`                  long
+       `x = 100` named, `(99+1)~objectName`               hundred
+       `y = 101` named, `(100+1)~objectName`              a String
+
+     Arrays, stems and variable references take a name
+     (corpus/lang/object_name_set_values.rex). The refusal is pinned by
+     `a_conversion_this_phase_does_not_model_is_loud_where_the_ones_it_models_answer`.
+
+ 37. SYSSTEMSORT WRITES ITS SORTED VALUES BACK BY TAIL, WHERE THE ORACLE
+     WRITES INTO THE STEM ELEMENTS IT CAPTURED (Phase 9b1). OWNER: none.
+
+     Licensed with the 9b1 spec, section 2 ("L12 is not fixed"), approved by
+     Moritz 2026-10-14. `StemClass::sort` finds each element's compound
+     variable and asks for its string value, in tail order, and after the
+     sort assigns the sorted values to those variables
+     (/home/moritz/dev/repos/ooRexx/interpreter/classes/StemClass.cpp:1659-1680
+     and :1730-1736). A `STRING` method run by the last element can empty the
+     stem after every variable is found. The oracle then writes into
+     variables the stem no longer holds, and the stem stays empty. This crate
+     assigns the sorted values to the stem by tail.
+     Measured, probe `stem_sort_emptied.rex` in
+     docs/superpowers/records/2026-10-14-phase-9b1/task-1/probes/, 3 runs a
+     side, fresh empty directory, rc 0 on both, oracle then this crate:
+
+       `say s.0 '['s.1'] ['s.2']'` after the sort   S.0 [S.1] [S.2]   S.0 [b] [c]
+
+     This is mutation of a collection during an operation over it. Matching
+     the oracle needs compound variables with an identity of their own, which
+     is representation (.superpowers/sdd/2026-10-14-phase-9b1/scout-leaks.md,
+     L12). With the `STRING` element first, the next element is gone when
+     the sort looks for it, and both raise 40.924 under `SIGNAL ON SYNTAX`.
+     Not witnessed in the corpus.
+
 EXPRKIND OWNERSHIP -- the expression forms 4a/4b do not evaluate
 ---------------------------------------------------------------------
 
 Criterion 5 requires every out-of-scope `ExprKind` variant to name an owning
 phase, and that owner has to be one of the phases the split table names --
 "4b", "4c", "Phase 5" or "Phase 7", spelled exactly as the table spells them,
 because `coverage.rs` and `loud.rs` assert the set and compare the strings.
 The set is the design document's and stays as that document wrote it; what is
 in force is narrower, and `owners.rs` asserts it directly -- no instruction
 tag names Phase 7 now, and no builtin exclusion may.
